n so  what i decided to do  was go to the jewish archives and you  must say to yourself well that seems  like a pretty obvious thing but i was  amazed  in my travels  american jewish historical society  american jewish committee american  jewish archives jewish theological  seminary i was amazed to discover  the amount of  ferment and the amount of interaction  the amount of discourse that was going  on throughout american jewry of all  levels in all parts of the country  about ford's behavior  the main quandary can be summed up very  easily by  a brief quote from  rabbi henry pereira mendes who was the  head of congregation sheriff israel in  in new york  at the time of this tremendous scandal  he said the following  and this is the big quandary of the  jewish committee the jewish community  excuse me  to answer  feeds the vehemence of the attacks that  is to say ford's attacks  not to answer seems unmanly and gives  the enemies a chance to say our silence  acknowledges our guilt  so there you have the problem which i  try to deal with in the second half of  the book  about how to go up against somebody with  that kind of money and that kind of  public profile and how do you  as a  jewish leader as jacob schiff or lewis  marshall or cyrus adler or whoever the  case may be how do you  speak out without drawing more attention  to yourself  and creating a backlash on the other  hand  if you're quiet  you're rolling over and taking it that's  not good either  so it's through the the riches of the um  the american jewish archives that i  discovered  the um  ways in which the jewish community  was able to try to get together  and respond to ford and of course as  many of you may know in the end it was a  libel suit which caused him to apologize  to the jews in 1927  for his uh  outspoken vehement behavior  now to conclude and of course i can only  touch upon this here  there was a piece in the herald this  morning i believe it was a column  which i  i can't remember the 